7.a) Use HashMap to store key-value pairs. 

Aim:
To create and use a HashMap in Java to store key-value pairs.

PROGRAM :- 

import java.util.HashMap;
public class HashMapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HashMap<Integer, String> students = new HashMap<Integer, String>();
        // Add key-value pairs
        students.put(101, "Rahul");
        students.put(102, "Priya");
        students.put(103, "Amit");
        // Display HashMap
        System.out.println("Student Details:");
        System.out.println(students);
    }
} 

OUTPUT :- 

Student Details:
{101=Rahul, 102=Priya, 103=Amit}


7.b) Traverse map using entrySet(). 

Aim:
To write a simple Java program to traverse a HashMap using the entrySet() method and display its key-value pairs.

Program :- 

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
public class MapEntrySetExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a HashMap
        HashMap<Integer, String> student = new HashMap<>();
        // Add elements
        student.put(101, "Anil");
        student.put(102, "Bala");
        student.put(103, "Charan");
        // Traverse the map using entrySet()
        System.out.println("Student Details:");
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, String> entry : student.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println("ID: " + entry.getKey() +
                               "  Name: " + entry.getValue());
        }
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Student Details:
ID: 101  Name: Anil
ID: 102  Name: Bala
ID: 103  Name: Charan


7.C) Sort objects using Comparable interface

Aim:
To write a Java program to sort objects using the Comparable interface.

Program :- 

import java.util.*;
class Student implements Comparable<Student> {
    int id;
    String name;

    // Constructor
    Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    // Compare students by ID
    public int compareTo(Student s) {
        return this.id - s.id;
    }
}

public class ComparableDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

        students.add(new Student(103, "Ravi"));
        students.add(new Student(101, "Anil"));
        students.add(new Student(102, "Priya"));

        // Sort the objects
        Collections.sort(students);

        // Display sorted list
        System.out.println("Students after sorting:");
        for (Student s : students) {
            System.out.println(s.id + " " + s.name);
        }
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Students after sorting:
101 Anil
102 Priya
103 Ravi

7.d)  Sort objects using Comparator interface.

Aim
To write a simple Java program to sort objects using the Comparator interface.

Program :- 

import java.util.*;
// Student class
class Student {
    int id;
    String name;
    Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }
}
// Comparator class to sort by Name
class NameComparator implements Comparator<Student> {
    public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
        return s1.name.compareTo(s2.name);
    }
}
public class ComparatorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Student> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add(new Student(103, "Ravi"));
        list.add(new Student(101, "Anil"));
        list.add(new Student(102, "Priya"));
        // Sort by name
        Collections.sort(list, new NameComparator());
        System.out.println("Students sorted by Name:");
        for (Student s : list) {
            System.out.println(s.id + " " + s.name);
        }
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Students sorted by Name:
101 Anil
102 Priya
103 Ravi
